course: dictionary
lesson: 1749
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them tell you which meaning the writer wants. «Long classical pieces have several parts called movements.» Pieces and movements are clues that this sentence is about music, so classical means serious, traditional music here.
explain: The same word can mean something different in another sentence. Classical can also mean traditional and accepted for a long time, the standard way. Swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Custom is another useful word here. «Many classical audiences wait to clap until the end of a long piece» shows a custom, which means something people usually do. It is not a law, so nobody gets in trouble if they clap earlier.
fact: Classical can mean to do with ancient Greece and Rome, their languages, art and ideas.
fact: In music, classical music is serious, traditional music by composers like Mozart and Beethoven, played by orchestras.
fact: Classical can also mean traditional and accepted for a long time, the standard way, as in classical ballet.
fact: Strictly, the Classical period is the time of Mozart and Haydn.
fact: A custom is something people usually do, but it is not a law.
exercise: «Long classical pieces have several parts called movements.» What does classical mean here? | *Serious, traditional music by composers like Mozart | Brand new, made this year | Ancient Greek or Roman writing | Loud and fast dance music
exercise: «Classical ballet has been taught the same way for over a hundred years.» What does classical mean here? | *Traditional and accepted for a long time | Very quiet | Made of wood | To do with ancient Rome
exercise: Pupils study classical history to learn about ancient Greece and Rome. What does classical mean in classical history? | The standard way of doing things today | A small village by the sea | *To do with ancient Greece and Rome | Serious orchestral music
test: Many classical audiences wait to clap until the end of a long piece. Is waiting to clap a law or a custom? | *A custom, which people usually follow but is not a law | A rule written in every concert hall | A law that the audience must obey | Something only musicians are allowed to do
test: In science, classical physics is the older physics of Newton. What does classical mean here? | Happy and bright | *Traditional and accepted for a long time | Describing animals | Made of gold
exam: Which meaning does classical have in «A classical composer like Mozart wrote many symphonies»? | Ancient Greek or Roman | The standard way of doing things | *Serious, traditional music | Very old and broken
